---
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

const posts = (await getCollection("blog"))
    .sort((a, b) => b.data.date.valueOf() - a.data.date.valueOf())
    .slice(0, 5);
---

<Base>
    <h1>Flexicast QUIC over a real network</h1>
    <p>
        We are running a demonstration of{" "}
        <strong>Flexicast QUIC (FCQUIC)</strong>
        <sup>
            <a href="#ref-1">[1]</a>
        </sup>
        , an extension of QUIC and Multipath QUIC that allows one source to
        deliver the same content to many receivers over multicast, and fall back
        to unicast for receivers that in unicast only networks. The source run
        in the UCLouvain network, and is connected to the GEANT network through
        Belnet. GEANT has native multicast connectivity, but also provides AMT
        relays, which allows clients in non multicast networks to setup AMT
        tunnels and receive multicast content by relying on replication on the
        relay. Run a receiver by{" "}
        <a href="/receiver">following the instructions</a> to help us gather
        insights into Flexicast QUIC deployments over real networks.
    </p>

    <h2>Why</h2>
    <p>
        Interest in IP multicast is rising again, largely surrounding massive
        video live streaming, file downloads (such as video games){" "}
        <sup>
            <a href="#ref-2">[2]</a>
        </sup>
        <sup>
            <a href="#ref-3">[3]</a>
        </sup>
        , and uses in AI datacenters
        <sup>
            <a href="#ref-4">[4]</a>
        </sup>
        . IP multicast saves a lot of bandwidth when many receivers want the
        same data, but inter-domain deployments suffered from pricing and
        deployment difficulties. Existing reliable multicast transports assume
        an all or nothing scenario, wherein an application either transmits all
        of its content over multicast, or none at all. The{" "}
        <em>chicken-or-the-egg </em>
        problem has also plagued multicast deployments, where large scale
        multicast deployments do not exist due to the lack of applications
        requiring it, which in turn never develop due to the lack of usable
        deployments
        <sup>
            <a href="#ref-5">[5]</a>
        </sup>
        .
    </p>
    <p>
        Flexicast QUIC attempts to solve this issue by relying on multicast as
        the main transfer channel, but also using unicast connections for
        retransmissions, control messages, and as a backup transfer channel for
        receivers in non multicast networks. This demo goes a little bit
        further, we extended FCQUIC to support fallback to AMT tunnels, which
        handle tunnel creation and byte replication on the relay. This allows
        the source to send its data once to a group of receivers whose reception
        mode may either be multicast or unicast through AMT tunnels. Several AMT
        relays are located in the GEANT network, so native multicast replication
        is still happening inside of the network whenever clients from different
        regions connect to the source.
    </p>
    <h2>How it works</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>
            Each receiver opens a normal, encrypted{" "}
            <strong>QUIC connection</strong> to the source. This unicast path
            carries control traffic, acknowledgements and retransmissions.
        </li>
        <li>
            The source announces a shared <strong>multicast flow</strong> using
            a frame called <code>FC_ANNOUNCE</code>. The receiver then tries to
            join the source-specific multicast (SSM) group, and if the join
            succeeds, gets the data efficiently.
        </li>
        <li>
            Receivers acknowledge multicast packets on their own unicast path
            with the source. The source retransmits lost packets over unicast
            when a few receivers lost them, or over the flexicast flow when many
            did (though the latter is not currently implemented).
        </li>
        <li>
            When the multicast flow is idle, the source transmits{" "}
            <code>FC_PING</code> frames over it, so that receivers can detect
            broken or non functional multicast connectivity. The interval at
            which these frames are sent is announced in the{" "}
            <code>FC_ANNOUNCE</code> frame, and receivers set the threshold for
            broken multicat connectivity at three times this interval.
        </li>
        <li>
            If multicast packets never arrive after a join, or stop, the
            receiver opens an
            <strong> AMT tunnel</strong> (Automatic Multicast Tunneling{" "}
            <a href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc7450">RFC7450</a>)
            to an AMT relay in the GEANT network, which allows it to receive the
            multicast packets from the GEANT network, encapsulated in UDP.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The demo</h2>
    <h3>Source</h3>
    <p>
        The source server is located in the UCLouvain network, connected via a
        GRE tunnel to Belnet, the belgian education network operator, which has
        multicast connectivity with the GEANT network.
    </p>
    <p>
        There are two FCQUIC sources running, one with QUIC's PING frames sent
        on the multicast flow every 250ms, however this frame is ack-eliciting,
        which induces load on the source at high receiver counts. The
        <code>FC_PING</code> frame was therefore introduced, which is by default
        non ack-eliciting, but may carry information to identify a group of
        receivers that the sources wants to obtain ACKs for. The demo receiver
        uses the <code>FC_PING</code> source. But you may run the PING receiver
        with the <code>:ping</code> tag. The picoquic container will also
        connect to the PING source.
    </p>

    <h3>Multicast fallback for receivers in non multicast networks</h3>
    <p>
        Multicast connectivity with the GEANT network is possible at
        universities in Europe, but is unlikely in most case. Thankfully, GEANT
        has a set of AMT (Automatic Multicast Tunneling{" "}
        <a href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc7450">RFC7450</a>)
        Relays that enable clients in non-multicast networks to create on-demand
        unicast tunnels to receive multicast content.
    </p>
    <p>
        Our demonstration makes use of these relays to provide client initiated
        multicast fallback to AMT if the client notices it isn't receiving
        multicast data natively. This is parallel to the unicast fallback
        offered by Flexicast QUIC, in which a client that doesn't meet the
        group's minimum network quality is evicted and receives content through
        unicast only. This unicast fallback is also applicable for clients in
        non multicast network, but the latter use case not in use in this
        demonstration in order for the source to distribute all of its content
        via multicast, and rely on the network to reach clients, wherever they
        are.
    </p>
    <p>
        The AMT relays in the GEANT network all advertise the Anycast address
        <code>62.40.96.44</code>.
    </p>

    <p>
        <a href="/receiver">Run a receiver</a>
    </p>

    <h2>Latest updates</h2>
    <ul>
        {posts.map((post) => (
            <li>
                <a href={`/blog/${post.id}`}>{post.data.title}</a>{" "}
                <span class="muted">
                    {post.data.date.toISOString().slice(0, 10)}
                </span>
            </li>
        ))}
    </ul>

    <h2>References</h2>
    <ol class="muted">
        <li id="ref-1">
            L. Navarre, Q. De Coninck, T. Barbette, and O. Bonaventure, “
            <a href="https://doi.org/10.1145/3750832.3750834">
                Taking the Best of Multicast and Unicast with Flexicast QUIC
            </a>
            ,” SIGCOMM Comput. Commun. Rev., vol. 55, no. 2, pp. 2–12, Jul.
            2025.
        </li>
        <li id="ref-2">
            McGill, “
            <a href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/124/materials/slides-124-mops-side-mtg-using-multicast-for-large-scale-live-streaming-over-the-internet-01">
                Using multicast for large-scale live streaming over the Internet
            </a>
            ,” IETF 124 MOPS side meeting, 2025.
        </li>
        <li id="ref-3">
            S. A. (BT), “
            <a href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/123/materials/slides-123-mops-multicast-assisted-unicast-delivery-maud-introduction-00.pdf">
                Multicast-assisted unicast delivery
            </a>
            ,” IETF 123 MOPS, 2025.
        </li>
        <li id="ref-4">
            J. Zhang, W. Cheng, and K. Liu, “
            <a href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-zhang-rtgwg-multicast-requirements-gaps-aidc/01/">
                Requirements and Gap Analysis of Multicast in AI Data Centers
            </a>
            ,” Internet-Draft
            draft-zhang-rtgwg-multicast-requirements-gaps-aidc-01, Mar. 2026,
            work in progress.
        </li>
        <li id="ref-5">
            L. Navarre and O. Bonaventure, “
            <a href="https://doi.org/10.1145/3744969.3748403">
                Towards an Internet Deployment of Flexible Multicast QUIC
            </a>
            ,” in Proc. ACM SIGCOMM 2025 Posters and Demos, Coimbra, Portugal,
            Sep. 2025, pp. 137–139.
        </li>
    </ol>
</Base>
